    { q: "Describe the structure and function of keratin.", a: "Keratin is the protein that gives the skin, hair and nails their toughness and their waterproofing. It is made of intermediate filaments, which are rope-like structures assembled from keratin molecules. The keratin molecules have a central rod-like domain and globular domains, and the rod-like domains of two molecules wind around each other to form a dimer. The dimers assemble into tetramers, and the tetramers assemble into the intermediate filament. The filaments are connected by disulphide bonds between cysteine residues, and the number of disulphide bonds determines the toughness of the keratin. The hard keratin of the nails and hair has more disulphide bonds than the soft keratin of the skin. Keratin is also waterproof, which is essential for life on land, and it is constantly renewed as the outer cells of the skin are shed and replaced. Defects in the keratin genes cause fragile skin and hair disorders." },
    { q: "Explain how the structural proteins are organised in the extracellular matrix.", a: "The structural proteins are organised into complex assemblies in the extracellular matrix, the material that surrounds the cells and gives the tissue its shape and properties. The matrix contains not only the structural proteins but also the proteoglycans and the glycoproteins. The cells control the assembly by secreting the proteins in a specific order and by interacting with them through receptors on their surface. The proteoglycans are large molecules with a core protein and many sugar chains, and they attract water and form a gel that resists compression. The glycoproteins, such as fibronectin and laminin, are adhesive molecules that bind to the structural proteins and to the cells, and they help to anchor the cells to the matrix. The organisation of the matrix varies from tissue to tissue, with parallel bundles in the tendon, a meshwork in the skin, a network in the cartilage, and a mineralised pattern in the bone." },
    { q: "Describe the clinical features of Ehlers-Danlos syndrome and explain their mechanism.", a: "Ehlers-Danlos syndrome is a group of inherited conditions caused by defects in the genes for collagen or in the enzymes that modify it. The clinical features include fragile, stretchy skin that bruises easily and heals poorly, hypermobile joints that dislocate easily, and fragile blood vessels that are prone to rupture. The features reflect the importance of collagen for the strength and integrity of the connective tissues. The fragile skin is caused by the weakness of the collagen in the dermis. The hypermobile joints are caused by the weakness of the collagen in the ligaments and tendons. The fragile blood vessels are caused by the weakness of the collagen in the vessel walls. The condition illustrates the importance of collagen for the mechanical properties of the tissues, and the management is supportive, with the avoidance of trauma and the treatment of the complications." },
    { q: "Describe the clinical features of osteogenesis imperfecta and explain their mechanism.", a: "Osteogenesis imperfecta is a group of inherited conditions caused by mutations in the genes for type I collagen, the main collagen of bone. The clinical features include brittle bones that fracture easily, often with minimal trauma, blue sclera, and in some cases, hearing loss and dental problems. The brittle bones are caused by the weakness of the collagen in the bone matrix, which makes the bone unable to withstand normal mechanical stress. The blue sclera is caused by the thinness of the sclera, which allows the underlying choroid to show through, and it is a sign that the condition is a generalised defect of collagen. The hearing loss is caused by the abnormality of the collagen in the middle ear. The condition illustrates the importance of collagen for the strength of the bone, and the management is supportive, with bisphosphonates to increase the bone density and surgery to correct the deformities." },
    { q: "Explain how a deficiency of copper can affect the structural proteins.", a: "Copper is a cofactor for lysyl oxidase, the enzyme that catalyses the cross-linking of collagen and elastin. The cross-links are essential for the tensile strength of collagen and the elasticity of elastin, because they prevent the molecules from sliding past each other when the tissue is stretched. A deficiency of copper therefore impairs the cross-linking, and the result is weak collagen and weak elastin. The clinical features include fragile blood vessels, which are prone to rupture, and weak connective tissues. The condition illustrates the dependence of the structural proteins on the trace elements, and it shows that a deficiency of a single nutrient can affect the mechanical properties of the tissues. The treatment is to correct the copper deficiency, which restores the activity of lysyl oxidase and allows the cross-linking to proceed." },
    { q: "Explain how the structural proteins are used in medicine and industry.", a: "The structural proteins are used in a wide range of medical applications because of their biocompatibility, their strength and their ability to promote the growth of cells. Collagen is used as a suture material, a wound dressing, a cosmetic filler, a tissue engineering scaffold, and a drug delivery vehicle. Elastin and keratin are also used, although less widely. The proteins are also important as biomarkers, with the measurement of collagen fragments used in the assessment of bone turnover and the measurement of elastin fragments used in the assessment of arterial stiffness. The development of drugs that target the synthesis of the structural proteins is an active area of research for conditions such as fibrosis, in which the excessive deposition of collagen causes the tissues to become stiff and dysfunctional. The clinical significance of the structural proteins is therefore broad, extending from diagnosis to treatment." }
