    {
      q: "Consolidation and your final test.",
      body: `You have now followed the Gram-positive cocci from the Gram stain to the diseases and the control, and it is time to fix the whole in a single framework.

Your cognitive map for the Gram-positive cocci, in five lines.

The foundation is that the Gram-positive cocci are the spherical bacteria that retain the crystal violet and appear purple, and the two main genera are the Staphylococcus, which are catalase-positive, and the Streptococcus, which are catalase-negative.

The Staphylococcus aureus is the most pathogenic of the staphylococci, and it is the coagulase-positive, lives on the skin and the nose, and causes the skin and the soft tissue infections, the serious infections, the toxin-mediated diseases and the hospital-acquired infections.

The virulence factors of the Staphylococcus aureus include the toxins, such as the cytotoxins, the exfoliative toxins, the toxic shock syndrome toxin and the enterotoxins; the enzymes, such as the coagulase; and the surface proteins, such as the protein A.

The streptococci are classified by the haemolysis pattern and the Lancefield grouping, and the main pathogens are the Streptococcus pyogenes, which is the group A and causes the pharyngitis, the impetigo, the necrotising fasciitis, the toxic shock syndrome, the rheumatic fever and the glomerulonephritis; the Streptococcus agalactiae, which is the group B and causes the neonatal sepsis; and the Streptococcus pneumoniae, which causes the pneumonia and the meningitis.

The wider connection is that the Gram-positive cocci are the major problem in the hospital, and the control requires the combination of the infection control, the surveillance and the prudent use of the antibiotics.

Now your final test. A 45 year old man is admitted to the hospital with the fever, the cough and the chest pain. The chest X-ray shows the consolidation in the right lower lobe, and the sputum culture grows the Gram-positive cocci in the pairs. The organism is alpha-haemolytic, sensitive to the optochin, and lysed by the bile salts. The blood culture also grows the same organism. The clinical team diagnoses the pneumonia with the bacteraemia and starts the treatment with the antibiotics.

Question one: what is the diagnosis, and how do the laboratory findings support it?

Question two: why is the organism identified as the Streptococcus pneumoniae, and what is the role of the capsule?

Question three: what is the treatment, and what is the importance of the identification?

Work them through before reading on.

My answers. One: the diagnosis is the pneumonia caused by the Streptococcus pneumoniae, with the bacteraemia. The laboratory findings support the diagnosis because the sputum culture grows the Gram-positive cocci in the pairs, which is the characteristic appearance of the Streptococcus pneumoniae. The organism is alpha-haemolytic, sensitive to the optochin and lysed by the bile salts, and the combination of these findings identifies it as the Streptococcus pneumoniae. The blood culture also grows the same organism, which confirms the bacteraemia and indicates the invasive disease. Two: the organism is identified as the Streptococcus pneumoniae because it has the characteristics of the pneumococcus, including the alpha-haemolysis, the optochin sensitivity and the bile solubility. The capsule is the most important virulence factor, because it protects the organism from the phagocytosis and allows it to cause the invasive diseases. The capsule is also the target of the vaccines and the marker for the typing by the Quellung reaction. Three: the treatment is the antibiotics, and the choice depends on the susceptibility of the organism. The Streptococcus pneumoniae is usually sensitive to the penicillins, but the resistance is increasing, so the susceptibility testing is important. The identification is important because it confirms the diagnosis, guides the choice of the antibiotic, and allows the surveillance of the resistance and the serotypes. The patient should also receive the supportive care for the pneumonia and the bacteraemia, and the prevention includes the vaccination of the high-risk groups.

This closes the study of the Gram-positive cocci. From here the course continues with the other groups of the bacteria, including the Gram-negative bacilli and the other pathogens.`
    }
