    { q: "Describe the characteristics of the Parvobacteria and explain the importance of the group in medicine.", a: "The Parvobacteria are a group of the Gram-negative bacteria that are small, fastidious and pleomorphic, which means they can change their shape depending on the conditions. The name parvobacteria means the small bacteria, and the group includes the Haemophilus, the Bordetella, the Brucella and the other genera. The organisms are the coccobacilli, which are the shapes between the cocci and the bacilli, and they appear as the tiny Gram-negative cells under the microscope. They are the fastidious organisms, which means they require the special media and the specific growth factors, and some of them were once mistaken for the viruses. The importance of the group in medicine is the serious diseases that they cause, including the meningitis, the whooping cough and the brucellosis, and the difficulty of the treatment and the culture." },
    { q: "Describe the characteristics and the diseases of the Haemophilus influenzae.", a: "The Haemophilus influenzae is the most important of the Parvobacteria, and it is a Gram-negative coccobacillus that is the fastidious and requires the X and the V factors. It grows on the chocolate agar but not on the ordinary blood agar, and it is the non-motile and the oxidase-positive. It is classified by the type of the capsule, and the type b, which has the polyribitol phosphate capsule, is the most virulent and causes the invasive diseases, including the meningitis, the epiglottitis and the pneumonia. The non-typeable strains lack the capsule and cause the localised infections, including the otitis media and the sinusitis. The capsule is the most important virulence factor, and the organism also produces the IgA protease. The diagnosis is by the Gram stain, the culture and the antigen detection, and the prevention is with the conjugate vaccine." },
    { q: "Describe the characteristics and the diseases of the Bordetella pertussis.", a: "The Bordetella pertussis is the Gram-negative coccobacillus that causes the whooping cough, which is the serious respiratory infection that is particularly dangerous in the infants. It is the fastidious organism that requires the special media, such as the Bordet-Gengou agar and the Regan-Lowe agar, and it grows slowly. The organism is the aerobic and the non-motile, and it is the oxidase-positive. It produces the several toxins, including the pertussis toxin, the filamentous hemagglutinin, the pertactin, the tracheal cytotoxin, the adenylate cyclase toxin and the dermonecrotic toxin. The disease has the catarrhal, the paroxysmal and the convalescent stages, and the paroxysmal stage is characterised by the whoop. The diagnosis is by the culture, the PCR and the serology, and the prevention is with the acellular vaccine." },
    { q: "Describe the characteristics and the diseases of the Brucella.", a: "The Brucella is the Gram-negative coccobacillus that causes the brucellosis, which is the zoonosis that is transmitted by the contaminated animal products. It is the fastidious organism that requires the special media and the prolonged incubation, and it is the facultative intracellular organism that can survive and multiply inside the macrophages. The main species are the Brucella melitensis, the Brucella abortus, the Brucella suis and the Brucella canis. The disease has the acute form, which is characterised by the fever, the sweats and the malaise, and the chronic form, which is characterised by the persistent symptoms. The complications include the osteomyelitis, the spondylitis, the endocarditis and the neurobrucellosis. The diagnosis is by the culture, the serology and the molecular tests, and the treatment is with the combination of the antibiotics. The prevention includes the pasteurisation of the milk and the animal control." },
    { q: "Describe the laboratory diagnosis of the Parvobacteria.", a: "The laboratory diagnosis of the Parvobacteria is difficult because of the fastidious nature of the organisms, and it requires the special media and the specific conditions. The Haemophilus influenzae is cultured on the chocolate agar, which contains the X and the V factors, and the satellite phenomenon is the key finding. The Bordetella pertussis is cultured on the Bordet-Gengou agar and the Regan-Lowe agar, and it grows slowly, taking the several days to form the colonies. The Brucella is cultured on the special media with the prolonged incubation, and it requires the special precautions because of the risk of the laboratory-acquired infection. The laboratory also uses the alternatives that do not require the culture, including the direct detection of the antigens and the nucleic acids, and the serology. The PCR is the most sensitive and the most specific, and the serology is used for the Brucella. The combination of the methods is the best approach." },
    { q: "Explain the importance of the growth factors X and V for the Haemophilus influenzae.", a: "The Haemophilus influenzae requires the X and the V factors for the growth, and they are the important part of the culture. The X factor is the haemin, which is the heat-stable, iron-containing molecule that the organism needs for the synthesis of the cytochromes and the other iron-containing proteins. The V factor is the nicotinamide adenine dinucleotide, or the NAD, which is the heat-labile coenzyme that the organism needs for the oxidation-reduction reactions. The organism requires both the factors, and it is the reason why it grows on the chocolate agar, which contains the lysed red blood cells that release the factors, but not on the ordinary blood agar, which contains the intact red blood cells that do not release the factors. The satellite phenomenon, in which the Haemophilus grows only near the colonies of the Staphylococcus aureus, is the result of the V factor that the Staphylococcus provides." },
    { q: "Describe the vaccines that are used for the prevention of the Parvobacteria diseases.", a: "The vaccines that are used for the prevention of the Parvobacteria diseases are the Haemophilus influenzae type b vaccine and the Bordetella pertussis vaccine. The Haemophilus influenzae type b vaccine is the conjugate vaccine, in which the polyribitol phosphate polysaccharide is chemically linked to a carrier protein, and the conjugation converts the vaccine from the T-independent to the T-dependent antigen. The conjugation allows the vaccine to stimulate the T helper cells and to produce the high-affinity antibodies and the immune memory, and it is immunogenic even in the infants. The Bordetella pertussis vaccine is the acellular vaccine that contains the inactivated pertussis toxin, the filamentous hemagglutinin, the pertactin and the fimbriae, and it is combined with the tetanus and the diphtheria toxoids in the DTaP vaccine. The Brucella vaccine is the live attenuated vaccine that is used for the animals." },
    { q: "Explain how the pathogenesis of the Haemophilus influenzae, the Bordetella pertussis and the Brucella differ.", a: "The pathogenesis of the Haemophilus influenzae, the Bordetella pertussis and the Brucella differ in the invasiveness and the site of the infection. The Haemophilus influenzae type b is the invasive organism that crosses the respiratory epithelium, enters the bloodstream and reaches the meninges, where it causes the meningitis. The capsule is the key to the invasiveness, because it protects the organism from the phagocytosis and the complement. The Bordetella pertussis is the non-invasive organism that remains on the respiratory epithelium and does not enter the bloodstream. It causes the disease by the toxins that damage the cilia and the respiratory epithelium, and the result is the whooping cough. The Brucella is the facultative intracellular organism that enters the macrophages and survives inside them by preventing the fusion of the phagosome with the lysosome, and it causes the chronic infection. The three organisms illustrate the range of the pathogenesis." },
    { q: "Explain the importance of the prevention in the control of the Parvobacteria infections.", a: "The prevention is the important part of the control of the Parvobacteria infections, because the treatment is difficult and the complications can be severe. The prevention of the Haemophilus influenzae type b is with the conjugate vaccine, which stimulates the antibodies against the capsular polysaccharide and produces the immune memory and the herd immunity. The result is the dramatic reduction in the incidence of the meningitis and the other invasive diseases. The prevention of the Bordetella pertussis is with the acellular vaccine, which is the part of the routine childhood immunisation. The prevention of the Brucella is with the pasteurisation of the milk, the proper handling of the animals and the vaccination of the animals. The combination of the vaccination, the proper food handling and the animal control is the best approach, and it is the more effective than the treatment because it prevents the disease from the occurring." },
    { q: "Explain the clinical importance of the identification of the Parvobacteria.", a: "The identification of the Parvobacteria is clinically important because it determines the diagnosis and the choice of the antibiotic. The different organisms have the different susceptibilities, so the identification guides the treatment. For example, the Haemophilus influenzae is treated with the third-generation cephalosporins, the Bordetella pertussis with the azithromycin and the other macrolides, and the Brucella with the combination of the doxycycline and the rifampicin. The identification also allows the surveillance of the resistance and the tracking of the outbreaks, and it provides the information for the prevention and the control. The laboratory uses the Gram stain, the culture, the antigen detection, the PCR and the serology to identify the organisms, and the identification is the basis for the clinical management and the public health response. The prevention is the important part of the control, and the identification is the basis for the vaccination and the other measures." }
