      body: `You have now followed the mycobacteria from the characteristics to the diseases and the prevention, and it is time to fix the whole in a single framework.

Your cognitive map for the mycobacteria, in five lines.

The foundation is that the mycobacteria are the organisms with the waxy cell wall that contains the mycolic acids, which makes them acid-fast, resistant and slow-growing, and the Mycobacterium tuberculosis is the most important.

The pathogenesis of the tuberculosis involves the inhalation of the droplet nuclei, the survival inside the macrophages by preventing the fusion of the phagosome with the lysosome, and the formation of the granuloma with the caseous necrosis, and the organism can remain dormant and reactivate.

The diagnosis begins with the sample collection, particularly the good sputum, and the laboratory methods include the microscopy with the Ziehl-Neelsen and the auramine-rhodamine stains, the culture on the solid and the liquid media, and the molecular tests such as the Xpert MTB/RIF and the line probe assays.

The drug resistance is the major problem, with the MDR-TB and the XDR-TB, and the detection is by the phenotypic and the genotypic methods, and the non-tuberculous mycobacteria and the Mycobacterium leprae are the other important organisms.

The wider connection is that the prevention is the important part of the control, with the BCG vaccine, the screening and the treatment of the latent infection, and the infection control, and the elimination of the tuberculosis requires the combination of the measures and the coordination between the different sectors.

Now your final test. A 35 year old man presents to the clinic with the cough that has lasted for the three weeks, the night sweats, the fever and the weight loss. He has been living in the crowded conditions, and he has been in the contact with the person who has the tuberculosis. The sputum sample is collected, and the Ziehl-Neelsen stain shows the acid-fast bacilli. The Xpert MTB/RIF test is positive for the Mycobacterium tuberculosis and shows the resistance to the rifampicin. The clinical team diagnoses the multidrug-resistant tuberculosis and starts the treatment.

Question one: what is the diagnosis, and how do the laboratory findings support it?

Question two: why is the organism identified as the Mycobacterium tuberculosis, and what is the role of the acid-fast staining?

Question three: what is the treatment, and what are the public health measures?

Work them through before reading on.

My answers. One: the diagnosis is the multidrug-resistant tuberculosis caused by the Mycobacterium tuberculosis. The laboratory findings support the diagnosis because the Ziehl-Neelsen stain shows the acid-fast bacilli, which are the characteristic of the mycobacteria, and the Xpert MTB/RIF test is positive for the Mycobacterium tuberculosis. The test also shows the resistance to the rifampicin, which is the marker of the multidrug-resistant tuberculosis, because the rifampicin resistance is almost always accompanied by the isoniazid resistance. The combination of the clinical features and the laboratory findings is the definitive diagnosis. Two: the organism is identified as the Mycobacterium tuberculosis because it is the acid-fast bacillus that is detected by the Ziehl-Neelsen stain and confirmed by the Xpert MTB/RIF test, which detects the specific DNA of the organism. The acid-fast staining is the important because the mycobacteria have the waxy cell wall that retains the carbol fuchsin even after the treatment with the acid-alcohol, which is the definition of the acid-fastness. The staining is the rapid and the inexpensive method, and it is the first step in the diagnosis, but it does not distinguish the species, so the molecular test is needed for the confirmation. Three: the treatment is with the combination of the second-line antibiotics, including the fluoroquinolones, the injectable agents and the other drugs, and the duration is the eighteen to the twenty-four months. The treatment should be guided by the susceptibility testing, and the patient should be monitored for the response and the side effects. The public health measures include the notification of the case, the identification and the screening of the close contacts, the treatment of the latent infection in the contacts, and the infection control measures, including the isolation of the infectious patient and the use of the masks. The patient should also be tested for the HIV and the other co-infections, and the adherence to the treatment should be supported.

This closes the study of the mycobacteria. From here the course continues with the other groups of the bacteria, including the spirochaetes and the other pathogens.`
