      q: "The chronic inflammation is characterised by the mononuclear cells. What are they, and what does each do?",
      body: `The chronic inflammation is characterised by the infiltration of the mononuclear cells, which are the cells that have the single round nucleus, and the main cells are the macrophages, the lymphocytes and the plasma cells. Each cell has its own role in the chronic inflammation, and the combination of them produces the tissue destruction and the fibrosis that are the hallmarks of the chronic response. The understanding of the cells is essential for the understanding of the chronic inflammation and the diagnosis of the chronic diseases.

My Socratic question: the macrophages are the central cells of the chronic inflammation, so what do they do, and how do they contribute to the tissue destruction?

The answer is that the macrophages are the central cells of the chronic inflammation, and they contribute to the tissue destruction in several ways. The macrophages are the cells that are derived from the monocytes in the blood, and they migrate into the tissue in the response to the chemotactic factors. Once in the tissue, the macrophages perform the several functions. They phagocytose the microorganisms, the dead cells and the debris, and they present the antigens to the lymphocytes. They release the cytokines, including the the interleukin-1, the the tumour necrosis factor and the the other mediators, which recruit the other cells and stimulate the response. They release the enzymes, including the the proteases and the the collagenases, which degrade the extracellular matrix and cause the tissue destruction. And they release the growth factors, including the the transforming growth factor-beta, which stimulate the fibrosis. The macrophages are therefore the central cells that produce the tissue destruction and the fibrosis, and they are the target of the many of the treatments.

The lymphocytes and the plasma cells have their own roles. The lymphocytes are the cells that are derived from the bone marrow and the the thymus, and they include the T cells and the B cells. The T cells are the cells that are involved in the cell-mediated immunity, and they release the cytokines that activate the macrophages and the other cells. The B cells are the cells that are involved in the humoral immunity, and they differentiate into the plasma cells, which produce the antibodies. The antibodies are the proteins that bind to the antigens and that contribute to the tissue damage in the autoimmune diseases. The plasma cells are the cells that are characterised by the eccentric nucleus and the abundant cytoplasm, and they produce the antibodies that are the markers of the chronic inflammation. The combination of the macrophages, the lymphocytes and the plasma cells produces the chronic inflammatory infiltrate, and the understanding of the cells is essential for the diagnosis.

Crucial insight: the chronic inflammation is characterised by the mononuclear cells, which include the macrophages, the lymphocytes and the plasma cells. The macrophages are the central cells, and they phagocytose, present the antigens, release the cytokines and the enzymes that cause the tissue destruction, and release the growth factors that cause the fibrosis. The lymphocytes include the T cells, which mediate the cell-mediated immunity, and the B cells, which differentiate into the plasma cells that produce the antibodies. The combination of the cells produces the chronic inflammatory infiltrate, and the understanding of the cells is essential for the diagnosis.`
