    { q: "What is an agranulocyte and how is it distinguished from a granulocyte?", a: "An agranulocyte is a white cell without visible cytoplasmic granules. It is distinguished from a granulocyte by three features: the absence of granules, a nucleus that is round, indented or kidney-shaped rather than lobed or segmented, and a cytoplasm that is a narrow rim rather than abundant and granular. The agranulocytes divide into two cell types, the lymphocytes and the monocytes." },
    { q: "Describe the maturation sequence of the lymphocyte lineage from lymphoblast to mature lymphocyte.", a: "The lymphocyte lineage begins with the lymphoblast, a large cell with a large round nucleus, fine lacy chromatin, one or two visible nucleoli, and a narrow rim of deep blue cytoplasm without granules. It passes through the prolymphocyte, in which the cell is smaller, the nucleus is denser and the nucleoli are disappearing, and the cytoplasm remains a narrow rim without granules. It ends with the mature lymphocyte, a small to moderately large cell with a dense round nucleus, no nucleoli, and a narrow rim of pale blue cytoplasm without granules." },
    { q: "Describe the maturation sequence of the monocyte lineage from monoblast to mature monocyte.", a: "The monocyte lineage begins with the monoblast, a large cell with a large round nucleus, slightly less lacy chromatin than the lymphoblast, and a greyish cytoplasm that may contain fine azurophilic granules. It passes through the promonocyte, in which the nucleus begins to indent and the grey-blue cytoplasm widens. It ends with the mature monocyte, a large cell with a kidney-shaped, indented or folded nucleus, grey-blue cytoplasm described as ground-glass, and the presence of fine azurophilic granules or vacuoles." },
    { q: "How does the laboratory scientist distinguish the lymphoblast from the monoblast?", a: "The lymphoblast has a deeply basophilic cytoplasm without granules, and a nucleus whose chromatin is finely lacy with one or two clear nucleoli. The monoblast has a slightly paler, greyish cytoplasm that may contain very fine azurophilic granules, and a nucleus whose chromatin is a little less lacy and often has a single, less distinct nucleolus. The two cells are the most easily confused in the marrow, so the laboratory scientist also uses the context of the surrounding cells, since the monoblast is usually seen near the monocyte precursors and is less numerous than the lymphoblast in normal marrow." },
    { q: "How does the laboratory scientist distinguish the mature lymphocyte from the mature monocyte?", a: "The lymphocyte has a dense round nucleus, a narrow rim of pale blue cytoplasm, and no granules or vacuoles. The monocyte has a kidney-shaped, indented or folded nucleus, a wider grey-blue cytoplasm described as ground-glass, and often fine azurophilic granules or vacuoles. The three features together distinguish the two cells, and the laboratory scientist looks for all three at once. The presence of vacuoles after some hours in a tube is a further clue to the monocyte." },
    { q: "Describe the correct performance of the differential count.", a: "The differential count classifies one hundred white cells on a stained film. The film is first scanned at low power to check that it is well made and well stained and to find the region in which the red cells are separated rather than overlapping or clumped. The count is performed in that region at oil immersion. The student moves across the field in a defined pattern so that no cell is counted twice, and classifies each white cell as a neutrophil, a lymphocyte, a monocyte, an eosinophil or a basophil. The result is expressed as a percentage of the total. The normal ranges guide the interpretation of the result." },
    { q: "State the approximate normal adult differential count and explain its clinical use.", a: "The normal adult differential is approximately forty to seventy per cent neutrophils, twenty to forty per cent lymphocytes, two to ten per cent monocytes, one to four per cent eosinophils, and less than one per cent basophils. The proportions are higher for lymphocytes and lower for neutrophils in children. The differential is used clinically to assess the response of the marrow to infection, inflammation or neoplastic disease, and any deviation from the normal range is a finding that guides the next investigation." },
    { q: "Explain the difference between a reactive and a neoplastic change in the white cell lineages.", a: "A reactive change is the response of the marrow to an infection or an inflammation, and it produces a raised count of normal-looking or activated cells of the appropriate lineage. An example is the activated or atypical lymphocytes seen in acute viral infection, which are large with abundant pale cytoplasm. A neoplastic change is the uncontrolled proliferation of a single abnormal clone, and it produces a raised count of cells that do not look like their normal counterparts. Examples include the lymphoblasts of acute lymphoblastic leukaemia, the abnormal mature lymphocytes of chronic lymphocytic leukaemia, and the monoblasts and promonocytes of acute monocytic leukaemia." },
    { q: "What are the laboratory features of acute lymphoblastic leukaemia on the blood film?", a: "The blood film in acute lymphoblastic leukaemia shows a raised white cell count with a majority of lymphoblasts. The lymphoblasts are large cells with a high nuclear-to-cytoplasmic ratio, a large round nucleus with immature chromatin, one or two visible nucleoli, and a narrow rim of cytoplasm without granules. The film often also shows a reduced number of normal red cells and platelets, because the marrow's normal haematopoiesis has been crowded out by the leukaemic cells. The presence of blasts in the peripheral blood is the hallmark finding." },
    { q: "How does the morphology of the blasts in acute leukaemia help to identify the lineage involved?", a: "The morphology of the blasts helps to identify the lineage because each lineage of blasts retains some of the features of its normal counterpart. Lymphoblasts have a narrow rim of cytoplasm without granules and a uniformly round nucleus with lacy chromatin. Monoblasts have a somewhat paler, greyish cytoplasm that may contain fine azurophilic granules, and their nuclei begin to indent as they mature into promonocytes. Myeloblasts have a narrow rim of cytoplasm that may contain fine azurophilic granules, and Auer rods may be seen in some cases. The distinction matters because the treatment and prognosis of the leukaemias differ according to the lineage involved." }
